KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ virtual base constructed once: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ default argument passed: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ base body reaches the virtual base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The hidden flag that selects base-object construction was appended right
after the written arguments of a mem-initializer, so it bound to the base
constructor's first defaulted parameter (`is_standard' became false only by
luck), and the flag kept its default `true': B was constructed as a complete
object and installed its own virtual pointer, through which its body found
the virtual base at B's own offset, inside D's buffer.  The default
arguments now come first.  Seen with the MSVC STL's istringstream
(cpp20_istringstream_string_allocator_delegation_msvc).
